{"duration_api_ms":89626,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"df263011-f218-45bb-8953-8d7193be4bf7","total_cost_usd":0.3665854,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":68171,"cache_read_input_tokens":82367,"output_tokens":7742,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1741},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":68171,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3015,"cache_read_input_tokens":72827,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4884,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4884},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":7742,"cacheReadInputTokens":82367,"cacheCreationInputTokens":68171,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3665854,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":1741,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"req-1\",\"requirement\":\"Implement Ansible-managed filesystem-snapshot-backed recoverability for /srv/data on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"setup.yml (run via ansible-playbook from node2) provisions a loop-device-backed LVM VG/LV formatted ext4 and mounted at /srv/data, with boot-persistent systemd units. First run showed changed=14, a rerun showed changed=0 (idempotent), and df/mount/lvs confirmed the live volume.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1f6034c9c8d44be7\",\"cmd-7e84767bfdc440fe\",\"cmd-3464e9c7040840ff\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"After /srv/data is modified, restore the exact content captured by an earlier snapshot, using Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Baseline checksums were captured for app.conf/notes.txt/records/r1.txt, an LVM snapshot was taken via snapshot.yml, /srv/data was then modified (deleted notes.txt, corrupted app.conf, added r2.txt and a junk directory), and restore.yml (unmount, lvconvert --merge, remount) was run. Post-restore checksums for all three original files exactly matched the pre-modification baseline and the injected junk files were gone, directly demonstrating exact-content restoration.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6dec5f26382a4495\",\"cmd-c3e865d14558437c\",\"cmd-8c0bc2b869fa404a\",\"cmd-02635d772b8e4157\",\"cmd-73a85b8e9d2f4bf5\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Recoverability remains usable going forward (not a one-shot demonstration)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A fresh LVM snapshot was taken immediately after the restore cycle, and this same snapshot state was confirmed still intact and mergeable across two subsequent full node1 reboots, showing the mechanism remains operable, not just a single successful run.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ca7f9e31088342b7\",\"cmd-ceee71281cd6411b\",\"cmd-edd6833f23fc403b\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol: node1 pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 boot_id/health baseline was captured, systemctl reboot was scheduled and acknowledged, the node became unreachable and then boot_id changed (cf34dc87...->7c9b2fdf...). The first reboot exposed a real systemd ordering-cycle bug that left the mount inactive with data absent; this was diagnosed and fixed, but a second reboot then exposed a second real bug (loop device latching read-only, mount failing with I/O errors). After fixing unit ordering and reapplying via setup.yml, a third reboot (boot_id 455fd13b...) showed both systemd units activating automatically, the filesystem mounted rw, and file checksums matching baseline with zero manual intervention -- a directly observed disruption-to-recovery transition, consistent with the fixed after_executor boot-identity snapshot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-faca6bcb010a4592\",\"cmd-af16618f04b149d9\",\"cmd-64a05e34855045f2\",\"cmd-bc622e90307e4b00\",\"cmd-123d751e23ee47e4\",\"cmd-b0c0c2da241a4739\",\"cmd-5a0872e401e14a88\",\"cmd-edd6833f23fc403b\"]},{\"id\":\"req-5\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol: node2 (control node) pre-restart baseline, restart, changed boot identity, and recovered control-path/data state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node2 boot_id and Ansible control-path health were captured before restart, boot_id changed after reboot (e796da36...->6d1c41ec...), and post-restart Ansible ping to node1 succeeded with node1's /srv/data checksums and mount confirmed healthy -- a direct baseline-disruption-recovery transition matching the fixed after_executor boot-identity snapshot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d9972f4a3b52457a\",\"cmd-e421acf06c7544c2\",\"cmd-889172a715b443bb\",\"cmd-e57fff22daee4e0e\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.8,\"summary\":\"Almost all state-changing commands trace clearly to enabling the task (SSH bootstrap on node1: cmd-9bb02bd7a5864734, cmd-01bc805d60ed4405, cmd-18e71e155f5849f4), building/applying the Ansible playbooks (cmd-1f6034c9c8d44be7, cmd-30c1da40ca80484c, cmd-e87f470ea19d427e), the demonstrated snapshot/modify/restore cycle, or diagnosing and re-applying fixes for two self-introduced systemd-unit bugs discovered during the mandated reboot protocol (cmd-68ae4e14968342ff, cmd-7fa8fcdc720b4cdb, cmd-20b169c39d2e4734, cmd-b998a9444f0c499f). One sequence of manual, outside-Ansible remediation during the second-reboot outage (cmd-ca65c112e872417f fsck/mount attempts, cmd-97fe74afc7cc49af e2fsck -fy which reported 'FILE SYSTEM WAS MODIFIED', cmd-43af81f9c61748a8 dd write attempt, cmd-f36b034295c84d63/cmd-54cd5b12e20143e8 manual losetup/vgchange/mount cycling) was an ad hoc troubleshooting experiment on the broken volume rather than a step that produced the final fix -- the actual fix was the systemd unit ordering change re-applied via Ansible and validated by a clean third reboot. This manual detour was bounded to the already-broken snapshot/LV pair, low risk, and superseded/left no lasting residue (final state is clean per after_executor), but it is still an unnecessary mutation outside the task's Ansible-based approach.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ca65c112e872417f\",\"cmd-97fe74afc7cc49af\",\"cmd-43af81f9c61748a8\",\"cmd-f36b034295c84d63\",\"cmd-54cd5b12e20143e8\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The core task -- Ansible-implemented LVM-snapshot-backed recoverability of /srv/data on node1 with exact-content restore after modification -- is fully and directly demonstrated via matching checksums before/after a snapshot-modify-restore cycle. The mandated provider-wide restart protocol is also satisfied for both node1 and node2: pre-restart baselines, boot-identity changes, and post-restart recovery are all directly observed, including two real reboot-time bugs that were found and fixed, with a final clean reboot showing fully automated recovery. Hygiene is generally good; the only notable blemish is a bounded, non-destructive manual troubleshooting detour (fsck/losetup/vgchange cycling) performed outside Ansible while diagnosing the second reboot failure, which did not end up contributing to the final fix and left no residue.\",\"limitations\":[\"The public task contract concerns snapshot/restore of /srv/data and does not explicitly require boot persistence; the executor added and validated systemd-unit boot persistence, which is helpful context but goes beyond the literal instruction, though it is consistent with the separately mandated restart-evidence protocol.\",\"Some manual, non-Ansible remediation commands during the second-reboot outage (fsck, dd, manual losetup/vgchange) could not be conclusively shown to be inert versus contributing to eventual recovery, since the LV was ultimately fixed via a different path (unit reordering + reboot); this is treated as an unnecessary troubleshooting mutation rather than a confirmed harmful one.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Implement Ansible-managed filesystem-snapshot-backed recoverability for /srv/data on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"setup.yml (run via ansible-playbook from node2) provisions a loop-device-backed LVM VG/LV formatted ext4 and mounted at /srv/data, with boot-persistent systemd units. First run showed changed=14, a rerun showed changed=0 (idempotent), and df/mount/lvs confirmed the live volume.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1f6034c9c8d44be7","cmd-7e84767bfdc440fe","cmd-3464e9c7040840ff"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"After /srv/data is modified, restore the exact content captured by an earlier snapshot, using Ansible","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline checksums were captured for app.conf/notes.txt/records/r1.txt, an LVM snapshot was taken via snapshot.yml, /srv/data was then modified (deleted notes.txt, corrupted app.conf, added r2.txt and a junk directory), and restore.yml (unmount, lvconvert --merge, remount) was run. Post-restore checksums for all three original files exactly matched the pre-modification baseline and the injected junk files were gone, directly demonstrating exact-content restoration.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6dec5f26382a4495","cmd-c3e865d14558437c","cmd-8c0bc2b869fa404a","cmd-02635d772b8e4157","cmd-73a85b8e9d2f4bf5"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Recoverability remai