-snapshot-backed recoverability of /srv/data on node1 with exact-content restore after modification -- is fully and directly demonstrated via matching checksums before/after a snapshot-modify-restore cycle. The mandated provider-wide restart protocol is also satisfied for both node1 and node2: pre-restart baselines, boot-identity changes, and post-restart recovery are all directly observed, including two real reboot-time bugs that were found and fixed, with a final clean reboot showing fully automated recovery. Hygiene is generally good; the only notable blemish is a bounded, non-destructive manual troubleshooting detour (fsck/losetup/vgchange cycling) performed outside Ansible while diagnosing the second reboot failure, which did not end up contributing to the final fix and left no residue.","limitations":["The public task contract concerns snapshot/restore of /srv/data and does not explicitly require boot persistence; the executor added and validated systemd-unit boot persistence, which is helpful context but goes beyond the literal instruction, though it is consistent with the separately mandated restart-evidence protocol.","Some manual, non-Ansible remediation commands during the second-reboot outage (fsck, dd, manual losetup/vgchange) could not be conclusively shown to be inert versus contributing to eventual recovery, since the LV was ultimately fixed via a different path (unit reordering + reboot); this is treated as an unnecessary troubleshooting mutation rather than a confirmed harmful one."]},"ttft_ms":24466,"type":"result","duration_ms":89824,"uuid":"c512d8b3-581a-4583-ba2b-b3e373db5563","ttft_stream_ms":2528,"time_to_request_ms":187,"first_content_frame_ms":2528,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
